{"authored" [{"question" "e-21715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x,$ then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/e52e49265a91cd725c6c5ffb89764144b14f9c7ecd39d8f72c4341337c985492.png) and  the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/04029e3a400b01eb4e063bc8609fc8724b1f9c0b0c0291194e330aead3dee346.png) property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/69fd9b3718fc120ca9106dcb8c7d74154071ba2454634d3b3a2cc59aa64a522c.png) and the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/819c819776622cbb5c29604cb7c5a6ef3bff8554912b649391b8b4f482aa36b0.png)." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21715/review.json" "file_sha256" "a7994bd41bf80f1272e8175bbccfebc1756305f974f947d53696b2780802ac49" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21715/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "50f16ef4cf7dde587903d1c623a0516db0f56417b20ecfbb5d8bc1acd7cecced" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21715/verification.json" "basis" 1506 "rationale" "The displayed proof labels L1 as e^x=O(1), L2 as sec(x)=O(1), L3 as f=O(2), and L4 as f=O(1), all as x tends to 0. The sum property combines L1 and L2 to obtain L3: e^x+sec(x)=O(1+1)=O(2). The constant multiple property applies to L3 with nonzero constant 2 to obtain L4. Citing L3 as a premise of L3 and L4 as a premise of L4 is circular and mismatches the formulas explicitly shown in the solution. Only the two highlighted premise labels are corrected; all formulas, the blank-box prompt, and source captures remain unchanged."} {"question" "e-21715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x), g(x), h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(k)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g = \\mathcal O(h+k)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad fg = \\mathcal O(hk)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(cg)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(f)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g) \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g+h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line  $\\textrm{L3}.$ From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\ne^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\textrm{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L1 and L2}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the sum property}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1+1) = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ From line $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L3}}$ we have\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $2$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the constant multiple property}}$ that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21715/review.json" "file_sha256" "a7994bd41bf80f1272e8175bbccfebc1756305f974f947d53696b2780802ac49" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21715/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "50f16ef4cf7dde587903d1c623a0516db0f56417b20ecfbb5d8bc1acd7cecced" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21715/verification.json" "basis" 1506 "rationale" "The displayed proof labels L1 as e^x=O(1), L2 as sec(x)=O(1), L3 as f=O(2), and L4 as f=O(1), all as x tends to 0. The sum property combines L1 and L2 to obtain L3: e^x+sec(x)=O(1+1)=O(2). The constant multiple property applies to L3 with nonzero constant 2 to obtain L4. Citing L3 as a premise of L3 and L4 as a premise of L4 is circular and mismatches the formulas explicitly shown in the solution. Only the two highlighted premise labels are corrected; all formulas, the blank-box prompt, and source captures remain unchanged."} {"question" "q-334055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-2x+1,$ show that $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist {{field-1}} constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le {{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the best upper bound we can form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & <{{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking {{field-7}} and {{field-8}} satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist $\\text{positive}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n|f(x)|\\le K|g(x)|\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n|f|\\le K\\cdot 1\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta \n$$\n\ni.e.,\n\n$$\n|f|\\le K\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|8{x}^{4}-2x+1| \\\\  & \\le |8{x}^{4}|+|2x|+|1| \\\\  & =8|x{|}^{4}+2|x|+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|8{x}^{4}-2x+1| \\\\  & \\le 8|x{|}^{4}+2|x|+1 \\\\  & <8{|\\frac{1}{2}|}^{4}+2|\\frac{1}{2}|+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & <3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking $K=3$ and $\\delta =\\frac{1}{2}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "389bad3a918409e6507ef083a82b655a9c08328ee3cf37bb2dc83d698fc5818e" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "positive"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course