n-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\delta =\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\delta =\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+x,$ show that $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist {{field-1}} constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le {{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<1.$ Then, we have {{field-6}}, and the best upper bound we can form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le {{field-7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 4|x|$ whenever $|x|<1.$ Thus, taking \n\n$$\n{{field-8}},\\,{{field-9}}\n$$\n\n satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist $\\text{positive}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n|f(x)|\\le K|g(x)|\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n|f(x)|\\le K|x|\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|3{x}^{2}+x| \\\\  & \\le |3{x}^{2}|+|x| \\\\  & =3|x{|}^{2}+|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $|x|<1,$ then we have $|x{|}^{2}<|x|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n3|x{|}^{2}\\le 3|x|.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le 3|x{|}^{2}+|x| \\\\  & \\le 3|x|+|x| \\\\  & =4|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 4|x|$ whenever $|x|<1.$ Thus, taking $K=4$ and $\\delta =1$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"