ence/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334064" "field" "field-9" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\delta =1"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used the fact that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1.$ Hence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, near $x=0$ we know that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x≈x,$ so $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ behaves like $\\frac{1}{x},$ which becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_