/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+2{e}^{2x})^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1+4{e}^{2x}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+4{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{2x})^{′}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{3x})^{′}}+0 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "389bad3a918409e6507ef083a82b655a9c08328ee3cf37bb2dc83d698fc5818e" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-