" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "the sum property"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "the transitive property"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the constant multiple property"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "a347cacd34141e7bf4c6f84915375b568cbc3e31ec2361ee497bceb7b9e388e8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x,$ show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-21710.json" "file_sha256" "1dabbf5fd592bf30131b25b56de391308f8a3f44588a6c9f29989d44822e5d9f" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|g(x)|}\n\\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < |x-a| < \\delta.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|x|}\n \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < |x| < \\delta}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|-3{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}+4x| \\\\  & \\le |3{x}^{4}|+|{x}^{2}|+|4x| \\\\  & =3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $|x| < 1,$ then we have ${|x|^2 \\lt |x|}.$ Also, since $|x|<1,$ we have $|x|^4 < |x|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n3|x|^4 \\le 3|x|\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n|x|^2 \\le |x|.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le 3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x| \\\\  & \\le 3|x|+|x|+4|x| \\\\  & =8|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)| \\leq 8|x|$ whenever $|x| < 1.$ Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and ${\\delta = 1}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-21710.json" "file_sha256" "1dabbf5fd592bf30131b25b56de391308f8a3f44588a6c9f29989d44822e5d9f" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-7762.json" "file_sha256" "6fb75942a794a87fb8e2e0cf8b93fb77eb9cd7d7974c3090c0b137ec18fe21a8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty.$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =1+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x.$  Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1/x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-7762.json" "file_sha256" "6fb75942a794a87fb8e2e0cf8b93fb77eb9cd7d7974c3090c0b137ec18fe21a8" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x,$ then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/e52e49265a91cd725c6c5ffb89764144b14f9c7ecd39d8f72c4341337c985492.png) and  the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/04029e3a400b01eb4e063bc8609fc8724b1f9c0b0c0291194e330aead3dee346.png) property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/69fd9b3718fc120ca9106dcb8c7d74154071ba2454634d3b3a2cc59aa64a522c.png) and the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/819c819776622cbb5c29604cb7c5a6ef3bff8554912b649391b8b4f482aa36b0.png)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-21715.json" "file_sha256" "51bb2f0fc24edb0782618d26e8f7f3de850d8b7992d46a659e8444f21ccbfdb6" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x), g(x), h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(k)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g = \\mathcal O(h+k)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad fg = \\mathcal O(hk)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(cg)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(f)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g) \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g+h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line  $\\textrm{L3}.$ From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\ne^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\textrm{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L1 and L3}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the sum property}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1+1) = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ From line $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L4}}$ we have\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $2$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the constant multiple property}}$ that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-21715.json" "file_sha256" "51bb2f0fc24edb0782618d26e8f7f3de850d8b7992d46a659e8444f21ccbfdb6" "basis" 1504 "verification" "/home/jake/D